<Base
  title="Kp, Bz, Solar Wind and Aurora Power: Reading the Numbers"
  description="What Kp, Bz, solar wind speed and hemispheric power mean for seeing the aurora australis, which ones matter most, and how to read them in a minute."
  path="/reading-the-numbers/"
>
  <h1>Reading the numbers</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Four readings do most of the work. Here is what each one is, which way is good, and how much weight to give it.
  </p>

  <SpacePanel />

  <h2>Kp: how big is the storm?</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      Kp is a planet-wide index of geomagnetic activity that runs from 0 to 9. The higher it goes, the wider the ring
      of aurora around each magnetic pole becomes, and the further north the southern lights reach. NOAA names the top
      levels on its G scale: Kp 5 is a G1 minor storm, Kp 7 a G3 strong storm, and Kp 9 a G5 extreme storm. Below
      that, NOAA calls Kp 0 to 2 quiet, Kp 3 unsettled and Kp 4 active.
    </p>
    <p>
      Kp is the number to compare against <a href="/kp-needed/">what your place needs</a>. Two cautions. It is an
      average over three hours, so it lags a sudden burst. And the forecast Kp is NOAA's best estimate: storms can
      arrive early, late, stronger or weaker than expected.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Bz: is the door open?</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      The solar wind carries a magnetic field. Bz is its north-south part, measured in nanotesla (nT). When Bz is
      negative, the field points south, opposite to Earth's own field, and energy from the solar wind gets in. When it
      is positive, most of that energy is turned away.
    </p>
    <p>
      So negative is good, and what matters is how far and for how long. A dip to minus 3 for five minutes changes
      little. Minus 10 held for half an hour is what aurora chasers wait for. The small chart shows the last hour, and
      the note beside the reading counts how many of the last 30 minutes were southward.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Solar wind speed: how hard is it blowing?</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      Speed is how fast the solar wind is passing the measuring spacecraft, in kilometres per second. This site labels
      under 400 slow, 400 to 500 average, 500 to 700 fast and above 700 very fast. Faster wind delivers more energy
      when Bz is south.
    </p>
    <p>
      The spacecraft sit about 1.5 million km towards the Sun, so what they measure has not reached Earth yet. NOAA
      puts the delay at under 30 minutes for a very fast wind near 800 km/s, and around an hour for an average one.
      The panel shows NOAA's own arrival estimate for the latest reading. That delay is your warning time.
    </p>
    <p>Density, the number of particles per cubic centimetre, is shown beside the speed for reference. The verdict does not use it.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Aurora power: how much is arriving?</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      NOAA's OVATION model turns the solar wind readings into an estimate of the total energy pouring into the aurora
      over each hemisphere, in gigawatts (GW), for a time 30 to 90 minutes ahead. The figure here is for the southern
      hemisphere.
    </p>
    <p>
      A widely used guide to the scale: below about 20 GW there is little or nothing to see, from 20 to 50 GW you need
      to be close to the aurora, and above 50 GW there is plenty of activity. Readings of 100 GW or more go with major
      storms. It measures the whole ring, not your patch of sky, so it tells you the aurora is strong, not that it has
      reached you.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Which to trust when they disagree</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Planning tonight:</strong> the forecast Kp for the dark hours, against what your place needs.</li>
      <li><strong>About to leave:</strong> the live Kp. If it is short of the forecast, believe the live value.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Standing in the dark:</strong> Bz and speed. A strongly southward Bz with fast wind means things are
        likely to pick up within the hour, even if Kp has not moved yet. Bz swinging north means a lull.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Then there is the part that has nothing to do with space: cloud, darkness and the moon. Each place page checks
      those hour by hour. <a href="/how-it-works/">How the go or no-go is put together</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
